---
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import Checks from "../components/home/Checks.astro";
import Field from "../components/home/Field.astro";
import Logos from "../components/home/Logos.astro";
import Wordmark from "../components/home/Wordmark.astro";
import Layout from "../layouts/HomeLayout.astro";
import { EMAIL, MAILTO, mailtoAbout } from "../lib/brand";
import { offers } from "../lib/offers";
import Portrait from "../photos/max-avatar.jpg";

// The headline's last word rotates through these. The first is the one that
// screen readers, no-JS and reduced-motion visitors get.
const words = ["control", "sight", "hold", "command"];

const method = [
  {
    title: "Decide what has to stay true.",
    body: "Before an agent writes anything, we write down the rules: what the product must do, the edge cases, and what “correct” means in numbers. This is the part you read, and it stays yours.",
  },
  {
    title: "Pick a shape you can hold in your head.",
    body: "Architecture and stack are chosen so the system stays legible to you and easy for an agent to work in: few moving parts, clear boundaries, boring technology. You can draw the whole thing on a whiteboard.",
  },
  {
    title: "Let checks do the reading.",
    body: "Tests, types and deterministic checks stand where line-by-line review used to. Every change an agent makes has to pass them and leaves a trail you can replay. You look at behaviour and results, not at lines.",
  },
];

const work = [
  {
    name: "Agentic coding",
    client: "LIQID",
    body: "Led the adoption of AI coding agents in the engineering team of a digital wealth manager.",
  },
  {
    name: "Lending platform",
    client: "Stryber",
    body: "A fintech loan management system with KYC, AML and e-signature integrations, where every step has to be documented.",
  },
  {
    name: "Web3 apps",
    client: "Showtime",
    body: "Consumer web3 products that generated more than $1M in revenue.",
  },
  {
    name: "Release tooling",
    client: "YNAP",
    body: "An internal Kubernetes release tool that handled around 430 releases a month.",
  },
  {
    name: "Expo Share Extension",
    client: "Open source",
    body: "An Expo config plugin for iOS share extensions with a custom view. 550+ stars on GitHub.",
    href: "https://github.com/MaxAst/expo-share-extension",
  },
];

const elsewhere = [
  { href: "https://www.linkedin.com/in/maximilianast", label: "LinkedIn" },
  { href: "https://github.com/maxast", label: "GitHub" },
  { href: "https://x.com/maxi4st", label: "X" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Ast: build products without reading the code"
  description="Ast is Maximilian Ast's independent studio in Berlin. I help founders and engineering teams build products with AI agents writing the code, new or rewritten, without losing their grip on the system."
>
  <Field />

  <main class="letter">
    <section class="hero">
      <a class="brand" href="#top" aria-label="Ast"><Wordmark class="wordmark" /></a>

      <h1 class="statement">
        <span class="line"><span>Stop reading the code.</span></span>
        <span class="line"
          ><span
            >Keep <span class="cycle" aria-hidden="true"
              >{words.map((word, i) => <em class:list={{ "is-on": i === 0 }}>{word}</em>)}</span
            ><span class="sr">{words[0]}</span></span
          ></span
        >
        <span class="line"><span>of the system.</span></span>
      </h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Ast is my independent studio in Berlin. I help founders and engineering teams build
        products with AI agents writing the code, new or rewritten, without losing their grip on
        the system and its architecture.
      </p>

      <a class="card" href={MAILTO} data-tap>
        <span>Still reading every line an agent writes?</span>
        <span class="card-go">{EMAIL} <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span></span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
      <p>
        AI writes code well, and it keeps getting better. Most teams still treat it like a junior
        developer: let it type, then read every line it produced. That keeps the pace of the old
        way and adds the cost of the new one.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is another way to work. You stop reading the code and put your attention where it
        counts: what the system has to do, how it is put together, and how you would know if it
        were wrong. The agent owns the lines. You own the system.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not vibe coding. Vibe coding gives up the understanding along with the code, and
        what you get is slop. Here you give up the lines and keep everything else: the
        architecture is yours, the rules are written down, and every change has to pass checks you
        trust.
      </p>
      <p>
        I did this with my own product. TaxMaxi began as a TypeScript monorepo. I rebuilt it from
        scratch in Go and htmx with agents writing the code, and I stopped reading it. It produces
        German tax reports, where every figure has to add up to the cent.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my experience, a codebase that agents started is far easier for agents to keep working
        on than one that people wrote. So starting again is often the faster way forward, and
        rewrites are easier than you&rsquo;d think. <em>I help you make that move.</em>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="approach">
      <h2 class="kicker">How I work</h2>
      <p class="statement statement-2">Rules first. <em>Then</em> speed.</p>
      <ol class="steps">
        {
          method.map(({ title, body }, i) => (
            <li>
              <span class="n">{i + 1}</span>
              <div>
                <h3>{title}</h3>
                <p>{body}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="offers">
      <h2 class="kicker">Ways to work together</h2>
      <ul class="rows offers">
        {
          offers.map(({ name, note, body }) => (
            <li>
              <h3>
                <a href={mailtoAbout(name)} data-offer={name}>
                  {name}
                  <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">&rarr;</span>
                </a>
              </h3>
              <p class="price" data-offer-note>{note}</p>
              <p class="row-body">{body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p class="aside">I do the work myself. The week on your system is the easiest place to start.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="work">
      <h2 class="kicker">I have built with</h2>
      <Logos />
      <ul class="rows work">
        {
          work.map(({ name, client, body, href }) => (
            <li>
              <h3>
                {href ? (
                  <a href={href}>
                    {name}
                    <span class="arr" aria-hidden="true">&nearr;</span>
                  </a>
                ) : (
                  name
                )}
              </h3>
              <p class="price">{client}</p>
              <p class="row-body">{body}</p>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
      <p>
        <a class="link" href="https://taxmaxi.com">TaxMaxi</a> is live, and it ships with an AI
        agent, a public API and an MCP server.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="about">
      <h2 class="kicker">Who I am</h2>
      <div class="person">
        <Image
          class="person-photo"
          src={Portrait}
          alt="Maximilian Ast holding his dog"
          widths={[96, 192, 288]}
          sizes="96px"
          quality={75}
        />
        <div>
          <h3>Maximilian Ast</h3>
          <p>
            Senior engineer and tech lead for about ten years, most of it freelance, in TypeScript
            and Go, across fintech, e-commerce, web3 and consumer apps.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        I like software that has an opinion and is a joy to use. Less is more, pragmatism beats
        ceremony, and you always know where a project stands. If something is a bad idea,
        I&rsquo;ll tell you before we build it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="contact" class="contact">
      <h2 class="kicker">Ready to stop reading the code?</h2>
      <p>
        Tell me what you are building, or what you would rebuild if you dared. I&rsquo;ll tell you
        honestly whether this way of working fits.
      </p>
      <p class="mail">
        <a class="statement mail-link" href={MAILTO}>{EMAIL}</a>
        <button class="copy" type="button" data-copy={EMAIL} data-tap>
          <span class="copy-idle">Copy</span>
          <span class="copy-done" aria-hidden="true">Copied</span>
        </button>
        <span class="sr" role="status" data-copy-status></span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <footer class="colophon">
      <Checks />
      <p class="colophon-line">
        <span>&copy; {new Date().getFullYear()} Ast &middot; Maximilian Ast, Berlin</span>
        <span class="colophon-links">
          {elsewhere.map(({ href, label }) => <a href={href}>{label}</a>)}
        </span>
      </p>
    </footer>
  </main>
</Layout>
